<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">identify</ansi>

Rare and better gear is found <ansi fg="yellow">unidentified</ansi>. You can see what it is, its tier, its
quality and its rarity, but not its affixes (<ansi fg="command">help affixes</ansi>). An unread item
gives only its base numbers, so there is always a reason to read it
before you commit to wearing it. Merchants buy unread gear at a discount,
priced by rarity alone (<ansi fg="command">help sell</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Ways to read an item</ansi>

  <ansi fg="yellow">A Scribe in your company</ansi>
  Your best Scribe reads gear for you (<ansi fg="command">help scribe</ansi>). At camp it is automatic
  and free; in the field it costs mana and you ask for it with
  <ansi fg="command">scribe [item]</ansi>.

  <ansi fg="yellow">A camp rest</ansi>
  Gear you and your companions are wearing when a camp rest finishes
  reveals itself, with no Scribe needed. Wear the item, rest, and you
  will know what it is.

  <ansi fg="yellow">A merchant, for a fee</ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">appraise [item]</ansi> at any merchant reads an unidentified item for gold:
  60 for a Rare, 150 for an Epic, 400 for a Legendary or Set. It reveals
  the item for good, so it is a way to spend gold on a find, not a toll
  you pay again (<ansi fg="command">help appraise</ansi>).

Uncommon gear shows its single affix from the start, and common gear has
none, so neither needs reading.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What rank covers what</ansi>

  Rank 1  Rare          Rank 3  Legendary and Set
  Rank 2  Epic          Rank 4  the same, and shows each affix's tier and
                                range, and where the item came from

Usage: <ansi fg="command">scribe</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">scribe [item]</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">scribe [member] [item]</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">autoskill scribe on|off</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">appraise [item]</ansi>
See also: <ansi fg="command">help scribe</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help rarity</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help affixes</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help camp</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help sell</ansi>.
